SUBJECT: Re : &NAME &NAME : further suggestions ( Fwd ) &NAME , I have responded to &NAME , just asking about the minimum number of copies they would require to print the proceedings , mainly to maintain the contact with them , in case things change . I am willing to check prices for binding etc. with local printers . If there is going to be a opinion poll on this , I suppose that the proceedings should mirror the philosophy of the conference ( papers from all areas in linguistics ) but the assesors should be pretty selective within each area , so that few papers from each area are chosen . &NAME On &NAME &NUM &NUM , &NAME East wrote : <QUOTE> Hi guys , Thanks all for your information-finding activities ! Though it 's disappointing that &NAME are n't interested ( and I do n't think we should press it as I do n't think it will do any good ) , we can at least move on and turn our attention to alternatives ... ... and getting them printed ourselves , using a local printers , seems to me to be the next option . I did n't know that you could buy &NAME numbers so this is good news & worth doing . ( We just might have some money left from the conference - if we do , the aim is to subsidise the proceedings so let 's hope . ) to do , then : * contact &NUM / &NUM local printers & get soft-binding printing costs ( for different numbers of pages ) . * cost the &NAME option ; list pros and cons - my worry is obsolescence * decide on no. of papers to be published . We have to decide whether our aim is to be a " journal worth &NAME " and therefore be more exclusive , or whether our aim is to provide a broader platform of opportunity for postgrad research and to have an " inclusive " feel . I think any decision to limiting the proceedings to &NUM or so would have to be taken in conjunction with the organising committee and maybe &NAME staff members . I do n't think it 's a bad idea , but so far the emphasis has been the other way , and the website & calls for abstracts have implied that we will try and publish papers that are accepted . Also , the conference this year has a wide scope , REALLY wide , in terms of the fields in which abstracts have been submitted ( whether or not this is a good idea we do n't know & will have to wait and see ) & the ethos is definitely towards an inclusive stance in this respect too . This time round anyway ! If we do exclude some papers from being published we must therefore * decide on how this is to be done - us & the camling team sitting in on different sessions ; &NAME staff involvement ? OK , if any of you are willing to contact printers let me know as I will summarise this correspondence to send to the rest of the committee & will also ask for volunteers . Also , &NAME , we will be looking for ( &NUM or &NUM ) editors-in-chief to co-ordinate the proceeding of the proceedings . The &NAME would also be welcomed onto the camling committee if wanted ( with no extra workload ) though I will also be liaising so this is not necessary . &NAME <QUOTE> Dear &NAME and &NAME I do n't think that we should write to them again and insist on an appointment . At the very least , we should wait a bit before doing so and see how feasible other options are for publishing the papers . Have a look at the e-mail I sent to &NAME ( East ) last night about the conversation I had yesterday with &NAME &NAME and &NAME &NAME , regarding other ways we might be able to publish the papers . I too thought that &NAME would refuse to publish the papers because of not making enough profit on them , but my e-mail contains a few other ideas for publication which would certainly be cheaper . I 'm also copying this to &NAME for info ( so you know who it 's already gone to ) and to &NAME &NAME ( because of the desktop-publishing bit ) . See you all soon ( ish ) - All the best &NAME &NAME I met &NAME &NAME and &NAME &NAME tonight ( at a reception , not specifically to talk about this ! ) and we had a little conversation about publishing stuff . I thought it 'd be useful to relay the results . Could you pass them on to those you think would find them interesting / useful ? I did n't want to clog up people 's inboxes by e-mailing them all directly myself . Thanks ! He said that insofar as there were any &NAME publications , they were published by &NAME , but that it was really quite a " loose " arrangement ; the " publication " that they have is the Journal of Linguistics but , though it 's the &NAME " official organ " , it is actually independent of them and would continnue to be published if the &NAME ceased to exist . It 's therefore questionable to what extent we can really say that " &NAME publications are done by &NAME " . He confirmed what I thought , that &NAME may well want to charge far more money than we can afford to publish our stuff , unless they " take us under their wing " ( his words ) and treat us nicely because we are students of the University etc etc. He therefore suggested &NUM alternatives : &NUM . Preparing camera-ready copy ourselves and taking it to a small local printer ; all they would therefore have to do would be print the copies and bind them but not produce them . This was , in essence , what &NAME &NAME suggested at our meeting . Apparently , &NAME was involved in something similar during his postgrad days in &NAME and it worked well for them then . There 's &NUM printer that I can think of off the top of my head that we could approach about this : &NAME &NAME and Sons , who offer a thesis-binding service through the &NAME &NAME . Their website address is on their carrier bags and I have one in my room but ca n't remember the address ! I 'll look next time I remember , unless anyone else beats me to it . &NUM . The &NAME idea that someone else had already had . It 's getting popular and I am becoming convinced . &NUM other points that both &NAME and &NAME made were : We ca n't possibly publish all the papers . We need to establish some kind of elimination procedure and , using that , select about &NUM at most to publish , whether on a &NAME or in print . This will help &NAME gain respectability ; people will get to know it as something that only publishes good papers , " a journal worth &NAME " in &NAME 's words . I have to say I instinctively agree with him and &NAME here . They also said that a website was definitely a good idea but that we should n't put on it any more than was in the printed / &NAME version ; so not all the papers there either . They thought that our task in deciding which papers not to publish would be helped by some of them being obviously not of publishable standard and by other presenters simply not sending us anything to publish after the conference . So perhaps we should n't chase them too hard . They thought that publications only had the ultimate cachet of respectability if they had an &NAME number . &NAME numbers are simply bought and attached to publications , and &NAME thought he could help with this . Apparently they can only be bought in batches of &NUM and he is going to have to buy some for a publication ( a corpus I think ) that he is involved in , but he wo n't need &NUM . Nor will we , he pointed out , so we could do a deal and buy one from him . I do n't know when he is going to buy his numbers ( but presumably it does n't matter for the minute anyway ) or how much they cost now ( in &NUM it was $ &NUM for &NUM ) , but it seems like a good idea to me . We can contact him about that when we need to . I think that this would apply if we were , in effect , publishing the proceedings ourselves , as I think &NAME numbers are obtained by the publisher . I think that 's all ! Sorry this is so long . See you soonish - all the best &NAME <END_QUOTE> <END_QUOTE> 
