SUBJECT: REMINDER : Linguistic Society talk this Thursday The &NAME University Linguistic Society will meet on Thursday &NUM &NAME , &NUM : Dr &NAME &NAME &NAME &NAME &NAME &NAME for English and &NAME &NAME , University of &NAME will talk on " &NAME production by language learners " ( abstract below ) &NAME : Thursday &NUM &NAME , 5.00pm ( tea served from 4.30pm ) &NAME : The &NAME Library , &NAME College , &NAME Street . Following the talk , the society 's officers will take the speaker out to dinner at a local restaurant ; members of the audience are most welcome to join us . Membership of the society for &NUM remains at &NUM pounds ( &NUM pounds for students ) . If not already paid , subscriptions can be brought to the meeting . Cheques should be made payable to &NAME University Linguistic Society . AGREEMENT PRODUCTION BY LANGUAGE LEARNERS Many domains of language processing ( e.g. , syntactic ambiguity resolution , anaphor resolution , phrase order selection ) require the co-ordination / integration of syntactic and non-syntactic information . In recent years , studies have begun to emerge which use standard experimental psycholinguistic and neurolinguistic research methodologies to investigate how native-like bilinguals and proficient second language ( L2 ) users " syntactic and non-syntactic processes in sentence comprehension are . The present study aims to extend this line of enquiry to subject-verb agreement in sentence production , another domain of language processing that has been argued to involve the integration of syntactic and non-syntactic ( e.g. , semantic / conceptual ) information . It examines whether proficient L2 English speakers show native-like sensitivity to syntactic and semantic / conceptual information in agreement implementation . This paper will begin with reviews of &NUM areas of research , the recent L2 processing literature which suggests that L2 users tend to have native-like non-syntactic processes but non-native-like syntactic processes in sentence comprehension and the ( L1 ) language production literature which demonstrates L1 speakers " sensitivity to syntactic and semantic / conceptual information in agreement implementation . &NUM parallel experiments will then be presented which were designed to probe whether proficient Spanish and Chinese L2 English speakers make use of syntactic and semantic / conceptual information in agreement production in their L2 . The results will then be used to address general questions about the development of L2 processing routines . 
