SUBJECT: Fwd : APPROVAL REQUIRED : mml-phd@lists.cam.ac.uk <QUOTE> The PITS - designed for and by graduate students engaged in language research * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * Hi all , This is just to remind you that the first PITS talk will be happening tomorrow , where we are delighted to welcome &NAME &NAME talking about a computational model for first language acquisition . Find all the details below . &NAME / coffee and biscuits will be served after the talk and we may migrate to the Anchor ... We would be grateful if you could forward this reminder to any graduate students you know who are interested / involved in language research . &NAME we get to meet you there ! Best wishes , &NAME and &NAME * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * &NAME &NAME Talk Series ( &NUM ) Friday &NUM January , &NAME &NAME &NAME &NAME , &NAME House , &NAME &NAME * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * &NAME &NAME from the NL&IP Group , &ORG will be talking about A Computational Model for First Language Acquisition * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * &NAME : Computational simulations can be a useful tool in the study of language acquisition . If a model of the learning procedure can be built then acquisition theorems can be investigated . The significance of such an investigation could be to add weight to the validity of a theorem or to highlight problem areas where the theorem will come unstuck . The model may also be able to add something to discussion in the event that there is dispute over which theorem is the most relevant for a particular phenomenon . I shall discuss a model of lexical acquisition has been developed in order to learn both the semantic and syntactic category of lexemes associated with a generalised categorial grammar . Computational simulation shows that acquisition is possible when some prior language knowledge is assumed . The effects on acquisition have been explored when aspects of this prior knowledge are varied . Any predictions made by the computational model are only as relevant as the original assumptions it makes . Bearing this in mind , the purpose of the work is to create an initial model of language acquisition and show that this model can indeed learn . This original model based on strong assumptions can then be made more complex ( the assumptions can be relaxed ) if the model promises to be useful . <END_QUOTE> 
