SUBJECT: Reminder - LINGUISTIC SOCIETY this afternoon The &NAME University Linguistic Society will meet on Thursday &NUM January , &NUM : Dr &NAME &NAME &NAME Department of Linguistics , University of &NAME will talk on " The meaning of markedness in &NAME &NAME " ( abstract below ) &NAME : Thursday &NUM January , 5.00pm ( tea served from 4.30pm ) &NAME : The &NAME Library , &NAME College , &NAME Street . Following the talk , the society 's officers will take the speaker out to dinner at &NAME &NAME ( &NAME ) on &NAME Road ; members of the audience are most welcome to join us . Membership of the society for &NUM remains at &NUM pounds ( &NUM pounds for students ) . If not already paid , subscriptions can be brought to the meeting . Cheques should be made payable to &NAME University Linguistic Society . THE MEANING OF MARKEDNESS IN OPTIMALITY THEORY The aim of this talk is to provide a formal account of the difference between " asymmetric " and 'symmetric " processes , set in &NAME Theory . A process is " asymmetric " in markedness terms if it always results in a particular feature value ( or one of a proper subset of possible values ) . For example , consonant epenthesis always produces a coronal or glottal segment , never a dorsal or labial . In contrast , 'symmetric " processes can affect any feature value ; for example , any place of articulation can undergo assimilation without any other also assimilating . This talk will present an account of the formal difference between asymmetric and symmetric processes . I will argue that the difference follows from the ( informal ) proposal 'More marked feature values are subject to greater preservation . " &NAME formally , symmetric processes are those for which faithfulness is relevant ; asymmetric processes are those for which faithfulness is irrelevant . This talk will discuss -- among other topics -- a typology of consonant epenthesis , and coronal neutralization in the Nepalese language &NAME . 
