SUBJECT: &NAME Institute of Language Research meeting , Monday &NUM December A MEETING of the Institute of Language Research will take place next Monday , &NUM December , at 3pm . You can find the agenda below under ( &NUM ) . The meeting will be followed by a TALK by &NAME &NAME of the University of &NAME at 4pm . The title is " The Transition to Language : Where &NAME , Culture and Evolution Meet " . You can find the abstract below under ( &NUM ) . Both the meeting and the talk will take place in the &NAME &NAME . Drinks will be served in the &NAME &NAME Room at 5pm . ( &WEBSITE , &NUM &NUM Minutes of meeting of July 1st . &NUM Matters arising . &NUM Student conference . &NUM Website . &NUM Postgraduate training &NUM &NAME . ( &NUM ) ABSTRACT The Transition to Language : Where &NAME , Culture and Evolution Meet &NAME &NAME &WEBSITE Understanding the evolution of human language is a uniquely challenging scientific problem . &CHAR truly explanatory account of the emergence of language must answer &NUM questions : why are languages the way they are and not some other way ? and how did language arise out of non-language ? The standard Evolutionary Psychology approach to these questions posits an innately specified strongly constraining device for learning language . The " why " question is answered by equating the set of possible languages with the set of learnable languages . Biological evolution through natural selection is often appealed to in order to answer the " how " question . The learnable languages are claimed also to be the optimal languages in terms of the survival / reproductive success of language users . In this talk I will argue that the Evolutionary Psychology view is flawed because it fails to take into account the complexity of the interacting dynamical systems that underlie human language . &CHAR new framework for understanding language evolution is put forward that explicitly models these interactions : the &NAME &NAME Model ( &NAME ) . The &NAME treats language as an emergent property of interacting " agents " whose behaviour is the result of observational learning of other agents " behaviour . &NAME , the input to a learning agent is previously learned behaviour by another agent . In computer simulations of iterated learning , linguistic structure emerges from randomness without natural selection . I will demonstrate this and some of the other features of the &NAME that have been the subject of recent research . For example : iterated learning gets round some long-standing learnability problems ; it shows why the " poverty of the stimulus " , far from being a problem for learning , actually enables the evolution of syntax ; it can explain why some languages have some stable irregulars within otherwise regular paradigms ; it predi cts certain &NAME constraints on linguistic variation ; it provides a new way of looking at the difference between creolisation and language change ; and it removes many of the obstacles in the way of an explanation of the origins of language in homo sapiens . &NAME &NAME &EMAIL / &ORG Theoretical and &NAME &NAME University of &NAME Dr &NAME &NAME University of &NAME Institute of Language Research &WEBSITE / 
