SUBJECT: &NAME : Constraint-Based Grammars Conference - &NUM / &NUM Dear all , The recently established regional Constraint-Based Grammar group will be meeting at the &ORG of the University of &NAME , &NAME on Wednesday , October 23rd . The meeting will be from 10am to &NUM pm and the schedule is the following : * &NUM : Tutorial Session : &NAME &NAME - University of &NAME An introduction to practical &NAME using constraint-based grammars # &NUM : coffee / tea pause * &NUM : &NAME &NAME - University of &NAME Japanese syntactic verb-verb compounding and grammatical information spreading in &NAME * &NUM : &NAME &NAME - University of &NAME &NAME &NAME Constructions : description and representation in an &NAME large grammar # &NUM : lunch * &NUM : Tutorial Session : &NAME &NAME - University College &NAME &NAME Grammar as a constraint-based theory of language # &NUM : coffee / tea pause * &NUM : &NAME &NAME - University of &NAME Getting a grip on combinators : &NAME &NAME Categorial Grammar * &NUM : &NAME &NAME - University of &NAME &NAME and &NAME # &NUM : business meeting / planning The meeting will be in &NAME and will take place in the &NAME &NAME Building , where the &ORG has found a new home recently . Details on how to get here can be found on &WEBSITE / . Once in the building , go to the reception ( just in front of the main entrance ) and you will get directed to room FW11 where the meeting will be held . Please distribute this message to all of those that you think might be interested . If you are planning to come , please let us know . Looking forward to seeing you here , &NAME &NAME and &NAME &NAME - local organizers &NAME : &NAME &NAME , Word Grammar as a constraint-based theory of language . I shall introduce the &NUM most general and controversial ideas of &NAME : &NUM That language is a ( conceptual ) network . &NUM That information is exploited by means of ( &CHAR ) spreading activation and ( &CHAR ) multiple default inheritance . &NUM That sentence structure is based on rich word-word dependencies rather than on phrase structure . I shall show how these ideas relate the goal of modelling human competence and performance , but I shall also consider how they relate to some of the goals of &NAME . &NAME &NAME , Getting a grip on combinators : &NAME &NAME Categorial Grammar In the &NAME Categorial Grammar ( &NAME ) framework , syntactic patterns arise as the result of the interaction of complex , directionally specified categories with a small set of rules that merge these categories together . While &NAME provides a theory of grammar in which nearly all &NAME variation is found in the lexicon , it nonetheless is frequently necessary to place language-specific restrictions on the applicability of the rules to ensure that ungrammatical sequences cannot be derived . In this talk , I will show how the more fine-grained conception of the categorial slash provided by the Categorial Type Logic tradition of categorial grammar can be utilized to rid &NAME of rule restrictions and endow the theory with greater explanatory power . I will show how the control that is obtained by this move simplifies previous &NAME accounts of English and Dutch and how it provides a &NAME account of syntactic extraction asymmetries in English , &NAME , and &NAME &NAME . &NAME &NAME , An introduction to practical &NAME using constraint-based grammars . Starting with a very brief overview of the state-of-art in &NAME , I will discuss the need for deep processing in some applications and motivate the use of constraint-based grammars for parsing and generation . I will mention some issues that arise when developing large grammars and briefly talk about semantic representation . Specific examples will be taken from the &NAME grammars and grammar engineering tools which are freely available and which have been used as components of several applications , including commercially deployed systems . &NAME &NAME , &NAME &NAME Constructions : description and representation in an &NAME large grammar The keystone of any &NAME application with decent to large coverage is arguably its lexicon . It is obvious that any such lexicon that would only contain simple words would undermine the overall quality of a system . Recent experiments were carried out to account for multiword expressions in the &NAME English Resource Grammar , a large-scale bi-directional grammar of English in the &NAME framework . &NAME &NAME Constructions are &NUM particular type of multiword expression that has special properties partly predictable . I will present them and provide a description of them very much based on a wider definition than traditionally used in the literature . In that perspective , I shall discuss how &NAME Verb Constructions can be represented syntactically as well as semantically and the advantages of such a representation for further processing . &NAME &NAME , Japanese syntactic verb-verb compounding and grammatical information spreading in &NAME Japanese has &NUM related verb-verb compound constructions . One is purely lexical , but the other &NUM ( syntactic compounds " ) show varying degrees of syntactic independence : the &NUM verbs ( &NAME , &NAME ) are distinct syntactic terminals . The syntactic compounds are classified into &NUM types by the properties of &NAME ( &NAME &NUM , &NAME &NUM ) , ( &NUM ) the unaccusative , ( &NUM ) the transitive and ( &NUM ) the serial verb type . The serial verb type forms a complex predicate in syntax , so that it is challenging to the framework like &NAME . The paper analyses the structural relations between &NAME , &NAME within &NAME and &NAME 's ( &NUM ) information spreading approach in &NAME . This architecture allows more flexible grammatical information sharing among c-structure nodes by dividing the information into naturrl classes . Under this proposal , &NAME clause is a sentential SUBJ of &NAME in the unaccusative type and an &NAME in the transitive type . In the serial verb type , &NUM verbs are serialised under the &CHAR node in c-structure and &NAME shares the only grammatical relations with its mother , namely the whole &NAME compound . Moreover , &NAME clause is a pseudo-complement , i.e. semantic argument , &NAME of &NAME . &NAME &NAME , &NAME and underspecification This paper suggests that the principles that regulate valence demands do not directly resolve the feature neutrality of heads or their dependents , but instead resolve feature neutrality , if at all , on the phrase that the head projects . Coordinate structures provide the clearest evidence for this position , though other constructions in which an element may participate in multiple valence constraints support a similar conclusion . This analyis preserves the useful properties of destructive strategies , while avoiding the conflicts noted by &NAME &NUM and others . An account along these lines contributes to a simpler and more transparent model theory for approaches such as &NAME . 
SUBJECT: &NAME &NAME of Language Research , &NUM December &NUM &NAME &NAME of the University of &NAME has agreed to give a talk to &NAME on December 9th at 4pm . The title is " The Transition to Language : Where &NAME , Culture and Evolution Meet " . You can find the abstract below . The talk will be preceded by a meeting of the Institute at 3pm . Both the meeting and the talk will take place in the &NAME &NAME . Drinks will be served in the &NAME &NAME Room at 5pm . &NAME &NAME &NAME Institute of Language Research &WEBSITE / &NAME The Transition to Language : Where &NAME , Culture and Evolution Meet &NAME &NAME &WEBSITE Understanding the evolution of human language is a uniquely challenging scientific problem . &CHAR truly explanatory account of the emergence of language must answer &NUM questions : why are languages the way they are and not some other way ? and how did language arise out of non-language ? The standard Evolutionary Psychology approach to these questions posits an innately specified strongly constraining device for learning language . The " why " question is answered by equating the set of possible languages with the set of learnable languages . Biological evolution through natural selection is often appealed to in order to answer the " how " question . The learnable languages are claimed also to be the optimal languages in terms of the survival / reproductive success of language users . In this talk I will argue that the Evolutionary Psychology view is flawed because it fails to take into account the complexity of the interacting dynamical systems that underlie human language . &CHAR new framework for understanding language evolution is put forward that explicitly models these interactions : the &NAME &NAME Model ( &NAME ) . The &NAME treats language as an emergent property of interacting " agents " whose behaviour is the result of observational learning of other agents " behaviour . &NAME , the input to a learning agent is previously learned behaviour by another agent . In computer simulations of iterated learning , linguistic structure emerges from randomness without natural selection . I will demonstrate this and some of the other features of the &NAME that have been the subject of recent research . For example : iterated learning gets round some long-standing learnability problems ; it shows why the " poverty of the stimulus " , far from being a problem for learning , actually enables the evolution of syntax ; it can explain why some languages have some stable irregulars within otherwise regular paradigms ; it predi cts certain &NAME constraints on linguistic variation ; it provides a new way of looking at the difference between creolisation and language change ; and it removes many of the obstacles in the way of an explanation of the origins of language in homo sapiens . &NAME &NAME &ORG &EMAIL Theoretical and &NAME &NAME &WEBSITE 
