ations , and between the &NUM . Although the paper is not about the capitalist system in general , it seems to be the case that trade liberalisation ( and the &NAME ) is the current favourite / popular objection to international capitalism ... Anyway , enough about that ! I hope you are well and that we will meet again soon perhaps this time in &NAME ? With much love , &NAME xxx &NAME " &NAME , &NAME " ( &EMAIL ) : <QUOTE> Greetings all : Yes indeed I quite enjoyed the trip down . The drive down was interesting enough and I did enjoy meeting you &NAME and seeing you again &NAME ( p.s. thanks for the use of the bed on the fourth of july ) . &NAME -- you ate more than a half cow ) . I survived the trip back -- it was n't bad at all . In &NAME -- the highways are absolutely horrendous and the lanes are narrow , as I 'm sure you can testify &NAME . I was falling asleep since I was running on barely any sleep for the entire week -- I was going to sleep based on &NAME time and waking up to &NAME &NAME ( where I do n't get much sleep as it is ) . I actually met up with &NAME &NAME in LA which was the night I had quite the drive in and out of LA to &NAME Beach while nearly falling asleep which was a little scary . <QUOTE> They are probably responding more to the money-grabbing attitude of " capitalists " than to the actual far reaching effects of the system as a whole . However I do sympathise with the notion that individual capitalists are not looking for the good of the whole but are ( necessarily ) trying to protect their own interests . While it may be the best option of those currently available , are there people investigating other options where the good of the whole is fundamental rather than a by-product ? <END_QUOTE> Does the end justify the means ? And does the nullification of the ends justify the evil that is being caused by the means ? The difference between " good " and " bad " is what is ultimately done with the profits and perspective . Certainly , there ar